  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nToday and Tonight... One final morning with a widespread freeze \nbefore we move into a warming trend for the rest of the week. \nSome additional below freezing temperatures possible, mainly in \nwestern Wisconsin Sunday night. This shift in temperatures today\nis driven by increasing southerly flow as the surface high \npressure moves out to the east. Today will likely see the first \n50s of the week, although widespread 50s will come later in the \nweek. Tonight the warmer lows will mainly be over western \nMinnesota where the main area of WAA is, while one more night of\nfreezing temperatures is forecast across western Wisconsin.\n\nMonday to Wednesday... This part of the week will be warm and\nwindy as we stay under the ridge aloft. High certainty in\nensembles and the NBM with highs in the 50s and lows in the 40s. \nAmple warmth in the lower atmosphere will favor mixing and \nbring down gusty winds of 20 to 30 mph in the afternoons during\nthis period. Precipitation chances remain near zero over this \nperiod under weak to non-existant synoptic forcing under the \nridge aloft.\n\nThursday into next weekend... This will be the period with the \nmore active weather. There is a low chance event Wednesday night\ninto Thursday morning and a high chance event late Friday into \nSaturday. This first low chance event is a shortwave trough \nmoving across the Midwest with more questionable moisture. This \nsecond chance will have both a larger scale wave aloft and much \ngreater moisture. This is what has NBM with slight chance (under\n30%) for this first wave and definite chance (over 80%) for the\nsecond larger wave. Still some uncertainty of the exact low \ntrack, but in most solutions we stay in the warm sector and rain\nis favored. As the low moves out and we get into the wrap \naround part of the system this is when cold air will return. \nThis will bring our warm up to and end and we could see some \nsnow mix in, especially in parts of central Minnesota. While \nsnow looks possible at the end, accumulations look unlikely at \nthis time.",
